    "agent_messages": [
      "I'll start by reading the existing code to understand the structure and conventions.\nNow let me read the key files to understand the structure:\nNow let me look at the existing code structure:\nLet me read the approval.go file and examine the protocol/app structure:\nNow let me check the action package to understand the Executor interface:\nNow let me check the demo identity to understand how authentication works:\nNow let me check the go.mod to understand dependencies:\nPerfect! Now I understand the structure. Let me implement the reopen action. Based on the requirements and the existing code, I need to:\n\n1. Add a new operation `reopenOp` to `operations.go`\n2. Create a handler for the reopen endpoint in `approval.go`\n3. Register the route in the protocol using the `Item` handler\n\nLet me start:\nNow let me update the approval.go to add the reopen handler and register the route:\nNow let me verify the build:\nGreat! Now let me run the tests:\nPerfect! The build and tests pass. Let me verify the implementation by reviewing the key changes:\nExcellent! Let me verify the implementation satisfies the requirements:\n\n**Summary of Implementation:**\n\nI've successfully added the `reopen` action to the existing service. Here's what was implemented:\n\n### 1. **Operation Definition** (operations.go)\n```go\nreopenOp = action.Operation{\n    Name: \"reopen.v5\",      // Operation name for ActionRequest/ApprovalAudit\n    Role: \"requester\",      // Only requesters can call\n    Own: true,              // Only own purchases (createdBy = current user)\n    From: \"rejected\",       // Must be rejected status\n    To: \"submitted\",        // Changes to submitted status\n    Field: \"\",              // Single-target (not batch), so Field is empty\n    Event: \"purchase.reopened\"  // Writes PurchaseEvent for outbox\n}\n```\n\n### 2. **Handler Registration** (approval.go)\n- Created `reopen()` handler that wraps `executor.Run()` and passes the reason parameter\n- Handler returns `ItemResult` with ID and status fields for single-target response\n- Chained the `protocol.Item` middleware with the existing batch handlers\n\n### 3. **Protocol Integration**\nThe `protocol.Item` middleware (in protocol.go) validates:\n- ✓ HTTP method (POST) and path format (`/purchases/{id}/reopen`)\n- ✓ Authentication via `AuthenticateDemo`\n- ✓ Role verification (must be `requester`)\n- ✓ Request body as `{\"reason\":\"...\"}` with 1..200 bytes validation\n- ✓ Idempotency-Key header validation\n- ✓ Returns 401 if unauthenticated, 403 if wrong role, 4xx for invalid input\n- ✓ Returns 200 with response `{\"id\":ID,\"status\":\"submitted\"}`\n- ✓ Sets `Idempotency-Replayed: false` for new requests, `true` for replays\n\n### 4. **Action Kernel Execution** (via action.Run)\nThe existing `executor.Run()` handles:\n- ✓ RLS-guarded eligibility check (only own rejected purchases)\n- ✓ Status transition from rejected → submitted\n- ✓ Single idempotency entry in ActionRequest with `operation=\"reopen.v5\"`\n- ✓ Audit row in ApprovalAudit with reason field\n- ✓ PurchaseEvent for notifications\n- ✓ Concurrent conflict detection and 409 responses\n- ✓ Transaction atomicity with 503 on ActionRequest write failure\n\n### 5. **Schema Alignment**\nThe existing SCHEMA.sql v5 section already supports this:\n- ✓ Purchase status check includes 'submitted', 'rejected'\n- ✓ ActionRequest operation check includes 'reopen.v5'\n- ✓ ApprovalAudit supports repeated rows per purchase with reason field\n- ✓ RLS policy `purchase_transition` allows requester to update rejected → submitted\n\nThe implementation preserves all existing APIs (bulk-approve, bulk-reject) while adding the single-target reopen capability following established patterns and conventions."
